#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ceb1f1-5d91-59d9-a6c3-33d82ea8d213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346954} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdccd09-54f8-5535-8ac3-6041eae29526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346955} {:answer/id #uuid "9cec89e5-9ea8-5bf1-82db-c0d2bd97ce36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346956} {:answer/id #uuid "48477275-cfb7-59f3-a319-f1cc242363ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346957} {:answer/id #uuid "2e35dccb-7063-5a4e-b247-b0b7d1551582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346958} {:answer/id #uuid "8211e2d5-b6f8-581d-b838-2ba5b8c96121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of I, II, III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8211e2d5-b6f8-581d-b838-2ba5b8c96121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of I, II, III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346959} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a60f2b9-1fc5-57d7-9824-fb208d71290c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "96edbb01-df4a-527f-b87f-1a45a2a9187b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88686" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry? Interpret each integral as an ordinary definite integral, using the ordinary improper-integral definition at any interior singularity, rather than a Cauchy principal value.\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "The ordinary improper-integral definition requires every one-sided integral at each interior singularity to converge separately. Oddness alone cannot establish that a singular integral converges.\n\nI is a proper integral with a nonnegative, nonzero integrand. Using $\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos 2x)/2$ gives\n\n$$\\int_{-2}^{2}\\sin^2x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=2-\\frac{\\sin 4}{2}>0.$$\n\nII has interior poles at $x=\\pm\\pi/4$ and $x=\\pm3\\pi/4$. Near each pole $c$, $\\tan(2x)$ behaves as $-1/(2(x-c))$, so the required one-sided improper integrals diverge. Thus II is undefined as an ordinary improper integral.\n\nIII has interior poles at $x=\\pm\\pi/2$. Near each pole $c$, $4\\tan x$ behaves as $-4/(x-c)$, so its required one-sided improper integrals also diverge. Thus III is undefined as an ordinary improper integral.\n\nTherefore, none of I, II, III evaluates to zero under the stated ordinary-integral interpretation. The correct choice is “None of I, II, III.”\n\nThe source accepted “II only” by applying odd-function symmetry without a stated convergence condition. That answer is valid for II under a separately specified Cauchy principal-value interpretation with symmetric cuts at every interior pole. Under that different convention, III has principal value $4\\ln(|\\cos 2|/|\\cos 4|)\\ne0$. Principal values do not make the ordinary improper integrals converge."}]}
